most sense.\n\nSo, I decide that I will get rid of T2 to pursue Half Flush.\n\n\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand contains two exposed melds of Character tiles ([W3, W4, W5], [W7, W8, W9]) and a hand consisting of [W4, W5, W6], a pair of F1 (East Wind), and isolated tiles J1, T2, T7. The most promising winning pattern worth at least 8 points is **Half Flush** (6 points), which consists of tiles from one suit (Characters) and Honors. Combined with potential minor patterns like **Pung of Terminals or Honors** (1 point, if F1 is punged), **Short Straight** (1 point, though definition varies, usually not applicable to 345/456), or **One Voided Suit** (if not subsumed), or simply by waiting for a **Self Draw** (1 point) or **Closed Wait** (1 point), Half Flush is the most viable route. This strategy requires discarding the Bamboo (T) tiles. Since \"Play T7\" is marked as invalid, **T2** is the next logical discard to clear the hand of unwanted suits while retaining J1 (Honor) which fits the Half Flush pattern.\n\nReason: The hand is structured for a Half Flush (6 fan) using Character and Honor tiles. To achieve this, the Bamboo tiles (T2 and T7) must be discarded. With T7 being an invalid move, discarding T2 is the correct action to advance the hand towards the scoring minimum.\n\nAnswer: Play T2"}
